# Sample size and CI width policy

## CI half-width reference (binary outcomes, σ ≈ 0.5)

```
half-width ≈ 1.96 · √(p(1−p)/N)
```

| N    | half-width (worst-case p=0.5) |
| ---- | ----------------------------- |
| 164  | 0.077                         |
| 541  | 0.042                         |
| 817  | 0.034                         |
| 940  | 0.032                         |
| 1172 | 0.029                         |
| 1319 | 0.027                         |
| 2048 | 0.022                         |
| 6511 | 0.012                         |
| 14042 | 0.008                        |

These half-widths are for *unpaired* estimates. Where the benchmark uses paired bootstrap (cross-cell), the effective variance is reduced and the relevant `Var(Δ)` should come from the bootstrap directly.

## Power for paired contrast

For paired design under matched items, assuming item-covariance:

```
Var(Δ) ≈ (1 − r) · 2 · p(1 − p),
```

where `r` is the per-item correlation between cells' scores. With `r ≈ 0.5`:

```
sd(Δ) ≈ √p(1−p)
```

For `p ≈ 0.5`, `sd(Δ) ≈ 0.5`. Mean Δ of 0.05 (5 pp effect) → z ≈ 0.1; need ~ N = (1.96 + 0.84)² · (sd)² / Δ² ≈ 12500 for 80% power at α = 0.05.

For Δ = 0.10: N ≈ 3125.
For Δ = 0.20: N ≈ 800.

## Practical implications

| Benchmark | N (effective) | minimal detectable Δ at 80% |
| --- | --- | --- |
| HumanEval | 164 | ~0.20 (paired) — practical just above this |
| IFEval | 541 | ~0.10 |
| TruthfulQA | 817 | ~0.08 |
| ToxiGen | 940 | ~0.08 |
| ARC-C | 1172 | ~0.07 |
| GSM8K | 1319 | ~0.07 |
| MMLU (subsample) | 2048 | ~0.06 |
| BBH (subsample) | 2048 | ~0.06 |
| MMLU (full) | 14042 | ~0.022 |
| BBH (full) | 6511 | ~0.035 |

## Sub-conclusion

